<PageShell page={page} h1="How Dubai property valuation works" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>What are the three methods, and when does each apply?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Comparable sales, income approach and DLD certification compared"
    columns={['Method', 'How the number is reached', 'Best for', 'Cost', 'Turnaround']}
    rows={[
      [
        'Comparable sales',
        'Recorded transactions for similar units, adjusted for floor, aspect and finish',
        'Setting or testing an asking price',
        'Free as offered here',
        'Within 24 hours',
      ],
      [
        'Income approach',
        'Achieved rent less running costs, capitalised at a comparable yield',
        'A tenanted unit or a short-let',
        'Varies by provider',
        'Varies by provider',
      ],
      [
        'DLD-certified valuation',
        'Inspection plus Land Department records, issued as a certificate',
        'Golden visa, mortgage, court, inheritance',
        'DLD fee schedule',
        'Set by the Land Department',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Method definitions as used by RERA-registered valuers in Dubai"
  />

  <p>
    The cost and turnaround columns are honest about what is not yet sourced. The Land
    Department publishes both for certification, and they belong in the table as figures rather
    than as a category:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="DLD certified valuation fee schedule and published turnaround" />
  </p>

  <h2>What happens, step by step?</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The building is identified, not the area.</strong> A community average is close
      to useless. Two towers a street apart can sit far apart on price per square foot, and
      averaging them produces a number that describes neither.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recorded transactions are pulled for that building.</strong> These are transfers
      that legally completed, with a date, a size and a price — the record of what was paid,
      not what was asked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Comparables are filtered to your layout.</strong> Same bedroom count, similar
      size, recent enough to describe the current market.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Adjustments are applied and stated.</strong> Floor level, aspect, finish and
      condition, each as an amount you can see and argue with rather than folded silently into
      a single figure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The service charge is priced in.</strong> A buyer pays it every year, so a high
      charge per square foot pulls down what they will offer for an otherwise identical unit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two numbers come out.</strong> A realistic asking price and a realistic
      acceptance price. A valuation giving only one is hiding the negotiation.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What do you need to hand?</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>The building name and unit type — the exact tower, not the community</li>
    <li>The internal size in square feet, as stated on the title deed</li>
    <li>The floor and aspect</li>
    <li>Current service charge per square foot, if you have the statement</li>
    <li>Whether the unit is vacant or tenanted, and the rent if tenanted</li>
    <li>For a DLD-certified valuation, the title deed and the owner’s identification</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is the rule on who may value a property?</h2>

  <p>
    Valuing a property for a transaction is a regulated activity in Dubai, which is why the
    certified route runs through the Land Department rather than through a brokerage. The
    governing text — the article and the authority — should be quoted here verbatim rather than
    summarised:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="verbatim regulation governing valuation as a licensed activity, with article number and authority" />
  </p>

  <p>
    What can be said without a citation is the practical consequence. Plainly publishes
    information about how valuation works and does not perform valuations. Where a valuation is
    produced for a reader, it is produced by a RERA-licensed partner, named with their own
    registration number on{' '}
    <a href="/property-valuation-dubai/golden-visa/">the golden visa valuation page</a> and on
    the valuation page itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Why do two valuations of the same property disagree?</h2>

  <p>
    Usually because they used different comparables. One valuer takes the last six months in
    the tower; another takes twelve months across the community. In a market that moved 13.6%
    over a year, the window chosen changes the answer more than the method does. Ask which
    transactions were used and over what period — a valuation that cannot answer that is an
    opinion.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If what you actually want is the number rather than the method, "
    anchor="a written valuation of your Dubai property"
    after=" is returned within 24 hours and lists every transaction it was built from."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
